WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.567
Some curves are easier to describe by a relationship than by one output formula

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:08.889
This circle contains an upper and a lower branch

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:15.533
Near suitable points, y can still depend on x even when we do not explicitly solve for it.

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:19.469
Differentiate both sides with respect to x

00:00:19.469 --> 00:00:22.105
The x squared term gives two x

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:30.767
Because y itself changes with x, the derivative of y squared is two y times y prime, an application of the chain rule.

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:36.561
Subtract two x, then divide by two y where y is nonzero

00:00:36.561 --> 00:00:43.304
At the point one, square root of three, the slope is negative one over square root of three

00:00:43.304 --> 00:00:46.300
The tangent slopes downward on the upper-right branch.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:54.208
At either horizontal endpoint, y is zero, so this rearrangement cannot produce a finite slope

00:00:54.208 --> 00:00:57.292
The circle has a vertical tangent there

00:00:57.292 --> 00:01:03.900
A failed division is useful information about the geometry, not permission to discard the condition.
